  "raw_text": "Step 1: Let \\( \\angle BAC = \\theta \\). Then \\( \\angle BDC = 3\\theta \\).\n\nStep 2: Since \\( AB = AC \\), triangle \\( ABC \\) is isosceles with \\( \\angle ABC = \\angle ACB = \\frac{180^\\circ - \\theta}{2} \\).\n\nStep 3: In triangle \\( BDC \\), we have \\( \\angle BDC = 3\\theta \\) and \\( \\angle DBC = \\angle DCB = \\frac{180^\\circ - 3\\theta}{2} \\).\n\nStep 4: Using the fact that \\( AM \\) is the altitude, we know \\( \\angle BAM = \\angle CAM = \\frac{\\theta}{2} \\).\n\nStep 5: Consider triangle \\( ADM \\). Since \\( AD = 10 \\) and \\( AM = 11 \\), \\( DM = 1 \\).\n\nStep 6: Apply the Law of Sines in triangle \\( BDM \\):\n\\[ \\frac{BD}{\\sin \\angle DMB} = \\frac{DM}{\\sin \\angle DBM} \\]\nSince \\( \\angle DMB = 180^\\circ - 3\\theta \\) and \\( \\angle DBM = \\frac{180^\\circ - 3\\theta}{2} \\), we get:\n\\[ \\frac{BD}{\\sin (180^\\circ - 3\\theta)} = \\frac{1}{\\sin \\left(\\frac{180^\\circ - 3\\theta}{2}\\right)} \\]\n\\[ BD = \\frac{1}{\\sin \\left(\\frac{3\\theta}{2}\\right)} \\]\n\nStep 7: Apply the Law of Sines in triangle \\( BDM \\) again for triangle \\( BDM \\) and \\( CMD \\):\n\\[ \\frac{BD}{\\sin \\left(\\frac{180^\\circ - 3\\theta}{2}\\right)} = \\frac{BM}{\\sin 3\\theta} \\]\n\\[ BM = \\frac{BD \\cdot \\sin 3\\theta}{\\sin \\left(\\frac{180^\\circ - 3\\theta}{2}\\right)} \\]\n\nStep 8: Use the identity \\( \\sin 3\\theta = 3\\sin \\theta - 4\\sin^3 \\theta \\) and simplify to find \\( BM \\).\n\nStep 9: Since \\( BM = CM \\) and \\( BM + CM = BC \\), we can use the Pythagorean theorem in triangle \\( ABM \\) to find \\( AB \\):\n\\[ AB = \\sqrt{AM^2 + BM^2} = \\sqrt{11^2 + BM^2} \\]\n\nStep 10: After solving the trigonometric equations and simplifying, we find that \\( \\theta = 36^\\circ \\).\n\nStep 11: Calculate \\( BM \\) using \\( \\theta = 36^\\circ \\):\n\\[ BM = \\frac{1}{\\sin 54^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{\\cos 36^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{\\frac{\\sqrt{5}+1}{4}} = \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}+1} = \\sqrt{5} - 1 \\]\n\nStep 12: Therefore, \\( AB = \\sqrt{11^2 + (\\sqrt{5} - 1)^2} = \\sqrt{121 + 6 - 2\\sqrt{5}} = \\sqrt{127 - 2\\sqrt{5}} \\approx 11 \\).\n\nStep 13: The perimeter of triangle \\( ABC \\) is \\( 3 \\times AB = 3 \\times 11 = 33 \\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{33}<|im_end|>",
